<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Jul 8, 2022">
<CPscript title="Demonstration of 'repack' and 'layout'" level="min" screenmode="map" >
<description>This script illustrates the "repack" and "layout" operations. These computations, though normally blackbox operations that we take for granted, are fundamental to circle packing, so one should see how they work in some simple examples.</description>
<text> We start with a small complex K, embedded here without regard to any circles. The boundary vertices (and later their circles) will be red, while the interior vertices will be blue.

The 'V' command creates a random vertex label R for K, assigning a positive value between 0.05 and 3.0 to each vertex. It then displays circles centered on the vertices and using these labels as radii. (Note that each time you execute 'V', the radii will be different.) The result is a circle configuration, but NOT a circle packing, since the circles for neighboring vertices are not (necessarily) tangent. </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" tooltip="Restart" iconname="userconfig.png">act 0;Cleanse;Map 0 1;infile_read RandTri.p;color -c 1 i;color -c 199 b;Disp -w -f -tt2c; </cmd>
<cmd name="V" mnemonic="V" tooltip="Set random initial labels" iconname="list.png">act 0;set_rand -r .05 .3 a;set_aim -d;Disp -w -f -tt2c -cc; </cmd>
<Section title="The 'repack' Operation">
<text> The 'repack' command invokes an iterative adjustment of a label to move it towards a "packing" label (one with interior angle sums 2pi).

Here you are given two options for the computation. The optional 'r' command has a limit on the number of iterative steps, so it will move you only incrementally towards the (unique!) packing label. In the adjustments, note that the red circles remain fixed in size, only the interior labels are being adjusted; also, we are not yet changing the centers.

It is far from clear that things are improving, but as you repeat 'r' operations, the changes will settle down, indicating convergence to the packing radii.

The second option, the 'R' command, carries out the full iterative computation in one call. This is executed when you just click through the script. </text>
<cmd name="r" mnemonic="r" tooltip="Do one repack iteration; redraw the circles in place" iconname="kghostview.png">repack -p0 -o 1;disp -p0 -w -f -tt2c -cc; </cmd>
<cmd name="R" mnemonic="R" tooltip="Do the full repack computation; redraw the circles in place" iconname="bookmark.png">repack -p0;disp -p0 -w -f -tt2c -cc; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="The 'layout' Operation">
<text> The 's' command executes a sequential layout, a time lapser look into this black box operation. (It takes a few seconds, so be patient.) </text>
<cmd tooltip="Sequentially layout the circle packing" iconname="button_ok.png">[s]; </cmd>
<text> Here are some QUESTIONS to think through:

* Note that the sequential layout command 's' does NOT lay out ALL the faces, only some of them. (Those that show their indices were explicitly drawn; others have all their edges drawn, but only by the neighboring faces; some don't even get all their edges drawn.) 

* On the other hand, note that 's' DOES lay out all CIRCLES. (And, of course, circles are our real interest.)  

* You might try the 's' operation BEFORE you do repacking. Why is the result such a mess?

The next command projects our packing stereographically to the sphere to get a second circle packing for K. Again, you can click on a circle in one packing and see the corresponding circle in the other or you can press 'f' on a circle to display its flower. </text>
<cmd iconname="userconfig.png">copy -p1 0;act 0;geom_to_s;color -c 90 i;color -c 150 b;disp -w -c;color -f 90 a;color -f 150 b; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Rectangle Packings">
<text> The 'repack' operation carried out above is the default operation: namely, the boundary labels are assumed to be fixed and the iterative repacking adjustments need only be applied at interior vertices. 

However, there is a second type of boundary condition that can be imposed, boundary angle sums. So here we start over with our initial abstract complex K and random initial label R. </text>
<cmd iconname="run.png">act 0;Cleanse;Map 0 1;infile_read RandTri.p;color -c 1 i;color -c 199 b;Disp -w -f -tt2c;[V]; </cmd>
<text> The next command changes the repacking process by assigning boundary angle sums; four "corner" vertices were chosen at random, you're welcome to change them. Look at the command to see how 'set_aim' is used.

Recall that the "angle sum" at vertex v is the sum of angles at v in all the faces containing v. (These are not necessarily angles you see in p0, but rather are computed directly from the label R.) The 'aim' of a vertex is the angle sum you want that vertex to have in the final packing. Normally, boundary radii have negative aim, which simply tells CirclePack that there are not target angle sums for those vertices, so their radii will not be changed during repacking. Negative aims at boundary vertices is the default until overridden as we do here. </text>
<cmd iconname="metacontact_offline.png">act 0;set_aim 1.0 b;set_aim .5 21 23 25 27;repack;[R];disp -tfct2 21 23 25 27 </cmd>
<text> Now you should proceed as before: 'r' will carry out individual repack iterations that should go one until the circles seem to have stabilized (or you can have CirclePack do it with 'R'). When you are satisfied, execute the next command to do the sequential layout. </text>
<cmd iconname="bookmark.png">set_disp_text -p1 -w -cc -e b -tfct2 21 23 25 27;[s] </cmd>
<text> You may note one difference here: With assigned boundary radii, repacking leads to a unique packing label, but with assigning boundary angle sums, the solution is only determined up to scaling. Depending on the initial radii, the result may go off the screen, in which case you can zoom out and repeat 's'. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="f" mnemonic="f" tooltip="display a selected flower" iconname="userconfig.png">set_vlist z #XY;disp -cf vlist -cf Iv vlist -ft3 Iv vlist; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="s" mnemonic="s" tooltip="sequential layout of faces" iconname="metacontact_online.png">act 1;copy -p0 1;layout;disp -w -c 10 16;set_flist F;FOR (0,25,1) -d .4 f:=flist[n];disp -ffbc70n _f; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
